<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><style>{{ css|safe }}</style></head><body>
<h1>Evidence-driven dimensionality reduction</h1><h2>{{ name }}</h2>
<p class="notice">Reporting mode: {{ mode }}. {% if mode == 'deterministic' %}This report is rendered from saved evidence by deterministic templates; no LLM API wrote this report.{% else %}An OpenAI API organized an extractive summary; every returned claim was checked against the supplied evidence.{% endif %}</p>
<h2>Dataset and declared scope</h2>
<p>Source: {{ profile.provenance.source|default('Synthetic numerical demonstration (fixed seed)') }}. The inspected source has {{ profile.n_samples }} observations and {{ profile.n_features }} numerical features. Sparsity: {{ '%.4f'|format(profile.statistics.sparsity) }}. The pilot contains {{ pilot_cohort.selected }} observations; the final analysis contains {{ cohort.selected }} of {{ cohort.available_post_qc }} available post-QC observations.</p>
{% if name == 'pathmnist' %}<p><strong>This is a stratified subset of the {{ profile.provenance.split }} split, not the complete PathMNIST dataset.</strong> Pixels are flattened RGB values; no pretrained neural features are used.</p>{% endif %}
<p>Class counts in the source: {{ profile.statistics.label_counts }}. Labels control stratified cohort composition and secondary diagnostic plots only. They do not enter preprocessing fits, embeddings, or primary selection.</p>
<h2>Preprocessing and rationale</h2>
{% for a in plan.actions %}<p><strong>{{ a.action }}</strong>: {{ a.reason }}. Parameters: {% for key, value in a.parameters.items() %}{% if key != "explained_variance_ratio" %}{{ key }}={{ value }}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}. Fit scope: {{ a.fit_scope }}.</p>{% endfor %}
<h2>Comparable pilot design</h2>
<p>Every method was attempted on identical saved input ({{ pilot_cohort.input_shape }}). MDS, GPLVM, t-SNE, and UMAP use repeated seeds; other methods run once. The metric reference is the same common PCA representation. Trustworthiness and mean k-nearest-neighbor recall use Euclidean distance and k={{ k }}; stability is mean pairwise seed neighborhood overlap. Quality = 0.45 trustworthiness + 0.45 neighbor recall + 0.10 stability. Deterministic stability is defined as one. Silhouette is secondary, excludes unannotated cells, and uses at most 2,000 observations.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Status</th><th>Trust</th><th>Recall</th><th>Stability</th><th>Quality</th><th>Seconds</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for r in pilot %}<tr><td>{{ r.method }}</td><td>{{ r.status }}</td>{% for key in ['trustworthiness','neighbor_recall','stability','quality','runtime_seconds'] %}<td>{{ '%.3f'|format(r[key]) if r[key] is not none else 'N/A' }}</td>{% endfor %}</tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table>
<h2>Prior suitability, empirical decisions, and failures</h2>
<p>Best pilot quality {{ '%.6f'|format(decisions.best_quality) }}; eligibility threshold {{ '%.6f'|format(decisions.threshold) }}. Retained methods: {{ decisions.selected|join(', ') }}. Low-confidence fallback: {{ decisions.low_confidence }}.</p>
{% for d in decisions.decisions %}<p><strong>{{ d.method }} — {{ d.decision }}</strong>. Prior: {{ d.prior_suitability }} Evidence and decision: {{ d.reason }}{% if d.pilot_evidence.warnings %} Warnings: {{ d.pilot_evidence.warnings|join('; ') }}{% endif %}</p>{% endfor %}
{% if summary %}<h3>API-organized validated evidence</h3>{% for sentence in summary.sentences %}<p>{{ sentence }}</p>{% endfor %}{% endif %}
<h2>Shortlist refinement and final results</h2>
<p>Only retained methods were tuned on the pilot cohort. The selection metrics are reused, including repeated-seed stability; ties at six decimal places favor lower runtime and then grid order. These are in-cohort descriptive metrics, not held-out generalization estimates.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Grid</th><th>Status</th><th>Quality</th><th>Seconds</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for r in tuning %}<tr><td>{{ r.method }}</td><td>{{ r.grid_index }}</td><td>{{ r.status }}</td><td>{{ '%.3f'|format(r.quality) if r.quality is not none else 'N/A' }}</td><td>{{ '%.2f'|format(r.runtime_seconds) }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table>
<p class="small">Final cohort planning: {{ cohort.size_justification }}.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Trustworthiness</th><th>Neighbor recall</th><th>Silhouette (secondary)</th><th>Seconds</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for r in final %}<tr><td>{{ r.method }}</td><td>{{ '%.3f'|format(r.trustworthiness) }}</td><td>{{ '%.3f'|format(r.neighbor_recall) }}</td><td>{{ '%.3f'|format(r.silhouette) if r.silhouette is not none else 'N/A' }}</td><td>{{ '%.2f'|format(r.runtime_seconds) }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table>
<h2>Interpretation and limitations</h2>
{% set top_trust = final|max(attribute='trustworthiness') %}{% set top_recall = final|max(attribute='neighbor_recall') %}
<p>On this final cohort, {{ top_trust.method }} has the highest observed trustworthiness ({{ '%.3f'|format(top_trust.trustworthiness) }}), while {{ top_recall.method }} has the highest neighbor recall ({{ '%.3f'|format(top_recall.neighbor_recall) }}). The first two PCA components explain {{ '%.1f'|format(100*(cohort.pca_explained_variance_ratio[0]+cohort.pca_explained_variance_ratio[1])) }}% of variance in the preprocessed feature space. These diagnostics describe different aspects of the embedding and need not identify the same method as best.</p>
<p>The shortlist reflects measured preservation of local geometry in the declared input representation under the specified compute budget. It does not establish a universally superior method. Changes in preprocessing, cohort composition, neighborhood scale, or objective weights can change the ranking. Linear PCA remains an interpretable baseline. Disconnected graphs and numerical warnings are retained in the evidence and affect eligibility.</p>
<p>GPLVM uses actual variational optimization with latent uncertainty and inducing GP variables. Its fixed budget does not prove convergence; loss history and latent displacement are saved. Successful-run runtime includes method imports, input loading and fitting, but excludes subprocess startup and common imports. Cold caches and hardware can affect timing. A conservative cubic bound informs PathMNIST cohort planning but is not a fitted complexity model. Biological and histological labels are diagnostics, not validation of causal or biological mechanisms. PBMC annotations derive from a prior processed clustering and are not independent ground truth.</p>
<h2>Figures</h2>{% for f in figures %}<figure><img src="{{ f.path }}"><figcaption>{{ f.caption }}</figcaption></figure>{% endfor %}
<h2>Reproducibility and sources</h2>
<p class="small">Configuration SHA256: {{ manifest.config_hash }}<br>Code SHA256: {{ manifest.code_hash }}<br>Git commit: {{ manifest.git_commit }}<br>Job: {{ manifest.job_id }}; host: {{ manifest.hostname }}; timestamp: {{ manifest.created_at }}.</p>
<p class="small">Software: {{ manifest.package_versions }}</p>
<p class="small">Source provenance: {{ profile.provenance }}</p>
<p>References: Scanpy PBMC3k dataset documentation; 10x Genomics PBMC3k. Yang et al., MedMNIST v2, Scientific Data (2023), doi:10.1038/s41597-022-01721-8. Titsias and Lawrence, Bayesian Gaussian Process Latent Variable Model, AISTATS (2010). GPyTorch GPLVM SVI tutorial. Full method and source links are in docs/METHOD_NOTES.md and docs/DATA_PROVENANCE.md.</p>
</body></html>
